with Microsoft revealing the specs for  project Scorpio we thought it would be  fun to build a PC inspired by the  upcoming console when we're done  building it we're going to benchmark it  to put it through its paces we're not  going to be able to recreate project  Scorpio exactly since Microsoft uses  heavily custom components and software  but the experiment is to replicate the  build as closely as possible using  off-the-shelf PC parts project Scorpio  uses a six teraflop GPU based on AMD's  polaris architecture considering AMD's  recently released RX 580 is a 6 point 1  teraflop graphics card that's also based  on porous we think it's a great fit for  our build here especially since both  GPUs are equipped with a gigabytes of  gddr5 memory CPU side-projects Scorpio  uses a heavily custom 8 core AMD SOC  that is based on the company's Jaguar ap  use unfortunately they don't sell a core  Jaguar ap use in stores but we think the  FX 8350 is a compelling option giving it  an 8 core CPU that launched around AMD's  Jaguar ap use Microsoft is equipping  Scorpio with a high-end vapor chamber  cooler the FX 8350 stock cooler is  anything but premium so we went with  Amy's higher-end  Wraith cooler for our motherboard we  went with gigabytes and 970 a - Duty 3p  which offers the am3+ socket support we  need it also offers high quality audio  capacitors which would hopefully get us  close to the high quality audio scorpio  is claiming to offer project Scorpio  will use 12 gigabytes of gddr5 memory  which is a speedy ram that's normally  relegated to graphics cards Microsoft  says 8 gigabytes of it will be for the  GPU because pcs can't use gddr5 as  system RAM we opted to use 8 gigabytes  of the slower 1600 megahertz ddr3 memory  which we think is a reasonable  compromise given our rig has to carry a  full on desktop operating system for  storage project Scorpio offers a one  terabyte hard drive so we're outfitting  our rig with one as well  project Scorpio we use a 245 watt  integrated power supply that Microsoft  asserts is very power efficient our EVGA  supernova PACU carries a higher 550 watt  output but it's also very power  efficient  with its 80 plus gold rating our mid  tower deep cool tesseract case will  probably be bigger than the scorpio but  hey at least it shares the same color  palette as the defcons considering  Microsoft is heavily touting project  Scorpio as a true 4k capable console we  wanted to test that claim with a  half-dozen games that are available on  both the PC and Xbox Microsoft worked  with developer turn 10 studios to show  off project Scorpio running an  unreleased build of 4-2 at 4k with a 60  FPS frame rate all we don't have access  to that build 2016's 4 - horizon 3 is on  the PC when we ran the racing game at  its ultra preset it achieved a 35  average FPS while it was probable it  wasn't until we cranked the settings  down to the low preset did we start to  hit 60 and for whatever reason we  started to see a lot of in-game  artifacting here it is important to note  that Forza horizon 3 with its open-world  setting may not be truly indicative of  the build that turn 10 studios recently  produced considering Gears of War is one  of Microsoft's leading exclusive  franchises it was a no brainer for us to  test Gears of War 4 when we ran the  game's built-in benchmark at its ultra  preset at 4k it achieved a choppy 27  average FPS when we cranked the preset  down to high it achieved a 37.5 average  FPS making it much more playable  considering the frame rate was pretty  consistent we can definitely see project  Scorpio running the game at 4k with  these approximate settings unfortunately  we weren't able to get the game running  consistently above 60 even on its lowest  preset it averaged just 53.8 overwatch  is not very graphically demanding when  we ran blizzards first-person shooter at  its ultra preset our rig averaged 64 FPS  though it did hit a low of 44 we can see  overwatch running in 4k at max settings  while at least something close to it on  project Scorpio with smooth frame rates  rise of the Tomb Raider is one of the  few piece of games at the moment that  supports Microsoft's DirectX 12 API when  we ran the games benchmark at its max  preset with four times anti-aliasing  enabled it Everage 12.2 FPS that's super  choppy when we disabled AAA and set the  game to its high preset our rig achieved  a 37 average FPS which is playable  we couldn't get the game to run  consistently above 60 though even as its  lowest setting it garnered a fifty point  eight average fps  Microsoft also asserts that Project  Scorpio is a console that's capable of  delivering high fidelity VR so we put it  through the steam VR benchmark here it's  scored a seven point four which is  actually considered high due to hardware  and software limitations you shouldn't  expect our project Scorpio inspired PC  to run exactly like Microsoft's upcoming  council we think there are enough  sensible similarities here that should  give us a rough ballpark figure of  performance analyzing the results of our  project Scorpio surrogate we can see  Microsoft's forthcoming system running  games at 4k though it's likely that all  games won't run at 60fps or we'll have  to make some graphical compromises along  the way do you think Project Scorpio  will be able to play games at 4k let us  know in the comments below if you liked  this video please hit the like button  and subscribe for more for a complete  breakdown of this PC build check out our  in-depth article on GameSpot calm thanks  for watching  [Music]  you
 

